atement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-155957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f9522b895dc6d7dcff4eb5a83a2549db062dfbe62841d36b23ba6e4cdb97767"} {"question" "q-155957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-155957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "All the statements are true"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-155957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "IV only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-156727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-156727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-156727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f9522b895dc6d7dcff4eb5a83a2549db062dfbe62841d36b23ba6e4cdb97767"} {"question" "q-156727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-156727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III and IV only"] ["text" "IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-156727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-157375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,3,-2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-157375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4a+4b \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ -2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(4a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ k(-2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ -2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-157375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f9522b895dc6d7dcff4eb5a83a2549db062dfbe62841d36b23ba6e4cdb97767"} {"question" "q-157375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-157375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "All the statements are true"] ["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I, II, and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044012/state.json" "file_sha256" "6f6c9a4e3e328d62fe160ed205cab7acff4ab2e33c79f74c9758bdcc6bdfbcd0"} {"question" "q-157375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "All the statements a